 descriptions and citations match the evidence trail, but two narrative claims go beyond what the grounded evidence supports.\n\n### Audit findings to fix\n- `threats-gain-attribution-1` (Discussion and Limitations (Threats to validity)): This asserts as fact a causal attribution of the 0.180-to-0.126 gain to library-grid matching. The manuscript says elsewhere that it cannot attribute the gain to any mapping or to the gate. No ablation was run, per-case baseline values are unavailable, and per-case gate decisions are unverified.\n  - claim: The library includes the exact case-6 sigmoid and saturating and count-based shapes close to the grid's other forms, so any gain over linear regression partly reflects a match between the library and the grid.\n  - evidence: paper.tex Limitations items (4) and (6) and the Case 6 paragraph in Results; paper_data.json ablation_studies is empty and evidence_sources.ablations status is missing; final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.candidate_diagnostics_trust is unverified; claims_grounding.json has no claim supporting attribution of the gain.\n  - requested_fix: Reword to a hedged possibility, for example \"the gain over linear regression may partly reflect the library containing shapes close to the grid's mechanisms; this was not tested\". Keep the statement that no ablation or per-case gate logging exists.\n- `design-after-search-percase-1` (Method (Frozen BIC-gated exposure library) and Limitations (1)): No claims_grounding.json entry or paper_data.json source records that the library author saw per-case model-selection errors. The same paper says per-case values for the model-selection plane are not part of the verified evidence and are not used. The grounded trace only shows that the three classes were scored sequentially (steps 1, 2, 3) and that only plane means are registered.\n  - claim: It was written after inspecting model-selection results ... the library was designed after seeing model-selection per-case errors\n  - evidence: paper_data.json improvement_trace (step_index 1-3, combined_score only) and grounding_sources result.improvement_001 to result.improvement_003 (plane-mean values only); claims_grounding.json has no claim covering this statement; paper.tex Model-selection paragraph says per-case values for this plane are not part of the verified evidence.\n  - requested_fix: Narrow both statements to what is grounded, for example \"the library was developed after the two earlier estimator classes had been scored on the model-selection seeds\". Drop the \"per-case errors\" wording, or add a grounded source for it.\n# Draft Reference Gap Memo\n\n**Status:** ready — three targeted retrieval needs.\n\nExisting coverage is adequate for design-based estimation (Aronow, Ugander, HT variants), GATE designs, and regression-based GATE estimation (Chin, Han and Ugander). Quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json` and are not flagged. The gaps below are the places where positioning or background statements are thinly supported.\n\n## Missing literatures\n- Exposure-mapping misspecification and unknown interference\n- Foundational interference/spillover and bias of naive estimators\n- Variance and confidence-interval estimation under network dependence\n\n## Targets\n\n1. **thesis-exposure-mapping-misspecification** (introduction, priority HIGH, positioning)\n   The central thesis is that fidelity of the fitted exposure mapping governs accuracy. The corner-extrapolation account in Results supports it. Only Aronow2013EstimatingAC is cited for \"exposure mapping\", and nothing covers misspecified mappings, unknown interference, or extrapolation to the all-treated/all-control corners (overlap/positivity). Reviewers would expect the paper to position itself against that work.\n   - Anchor: \"accuracy is governed by the fidelity of the fitted exposure mapping to the generating mechanism\"\n   - Search topics: misspecified exposure mapping bias under network interference; average treatment effects under unknown interference; positivity/overlap extrapolation for global effects.\n\n2. **intro-interference-dim-bias** (introduction, priority MEDIUM, background)\n   The first paragraph states that network A/B tests violate no-interference and that DiM is systematically biased for the GATE. These are uncited foundational claims; only HT estimators are cited there.\n   - Anchor: \"The difference-in-means (DiM) estimator, which is unbiased without interference, is systematically biased...\"\n   - Search topics: causal inference with interference/spillovers; bias of difference-in-means in network A/B tests.\n\n3. **reliability-interval-undercoverage** (results, reliability subsection, priority MEDIUM, comparator)\n   Coverage of 0.839 against a nominal 0.95 is a headline negative result. The draft explains that HC1 intervals ignore cluster dependence and use no resampling, but it cites no work on variance estimation or confidence intervals under network dependence.\n   - Anchor: \"ignores cluster dependence under the cluster design and uses no resampling\"\n   - Search topics: variance estimation under network dependence in interference experiments; cluster-robust or network HAC standard errors.\n\n## Not flagged\nThe related-work paragraphs on design-based estimation, designs, and regression adjustment are densely cited. The HT, graph-cluster-randomization, and Chin/Han comparators are covered. GNN/Facebook100 mentions appear only in limitations as untested extensions and need no added support.\nfigures:\nmain_accuracy.pdf\nmain_accuracy.png\nmain_accuracy.png.prov.json\noverview_schematic.png\noverview_schematic.png.prov.json\noverview_schematic.prompts.json\nreliability.pdf\nreliability.png\nreliability.png.prov.json\n\nscientific-evidence:\nbundle.manifest.json\nledger.jsonl\nschemas\nspec.md\nverification-report.json\n\nsource_evidence:\nestimator_01\nestimator_02\nevaluator\nfrozen_estimator","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutput