{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FEfLFapDYguPvLGjL8eaaN","type":"tool_result","content":"# Agentic evidence audit feedback\n\n- generated_at: `2026-11-11T09:01:56.230600+00:00`\n- ok: `False`\n- verdict: `fail`\n- finding_count: `2`\n- audit_json_path: `work/results/t09/claude/20261111_071431/paper/paper_evidence_audit.json`\n- audit_markdown_path: `work/results/t09/claude/20261111_071431/paper/paper_evidence_audit.md`\n- raw_output_path: `work/results/t09/claude/20261111_071431/paper/paper_evidence_audit_raw.txt`\n\n## Summary\nAll numeric results, source-code descriptions and citations match the evidence trail, but two narrative claims go beyond what the grounded evidence supports.\n\n## Findings\n- `threats-gain-attribution-1` (Discussion and Limitations (Threats to validity)): This asserts as fact a causal attribution of the 0.180-to-0.126 gain to library-grid matching. The manuscript says elsewhere that it cannot attribute the gain to any mapping or to the gate. No ablation was run, per-case baseline values are unavailable, and per-case gate decisions are unverified.\n  - claim: The library includes the exact case-6 sigmoid and saturating and count-based shapes close to the grid's other forms, so any gain over linear regression partly reflects a match between the library and the grid.\n  - evidence: paper.tex Limitations items (4) and (6) and the Case 6 paragraph in Results; paper_data.json ablation_studies is empty and evidence_sources.ablations status is missing; final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.candidate_diagnostics_trust is unverified; claims_grounding.json has no claim supporting attribution of the gain.\n  - requested_fix: Reword to a hedged possibility, for example \"the gain over linear regression may partly reflect the library containing shapes close to the grid's mechanisms; this was not tested\". Keep the statement that no ablation or per-case gate logging exists.\n- `design-after-search-percase-1` (Method (Frozen BIC-gated exposure library) and Limitations (1)): No claims_grounding.json entry or paper_data.json source records that the library author saw per-case model-selection errors. The same paper says per-case values for the model-selection plane are not part of the verified evidence and are not used. The grounded trace only shows that the three classes were scored sequentially (steps 1, 2, 3) and that only plane means are registered.\n  - claim: It was written after inspecting model-selection results ... the library was designed after seeing model-selection per-case errors\n  - evidence: paper_data.json improvement_trace (step_index 1-3, combined_score only) and grounding_sources result.improvement_001 to result.improvement_003 (plane-mean values only); claims_grounding.json has no claim covering this statement; paper.tex Model-selection paragraph says per-case values for this plane are not part of the verified evidence.\n  - requested_fix: Narrow both statements to what is grounded, for example \"the library was developed after the two earlier estimator classes had been scored on the model-selection seeds\". Drop the \"per-case errors\" wording, or add a grounded source for it.\n110:\\paragraph{Frozen BIC-gated exposure library (studied estimator).} The studied estimator (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/frozen\\_estimator/program.py}, functions \\texttt{candidate\\_designs}, \\texttt{bic\\_gate}, \\texttt{\\_estimate}) fits by least squares the linear model above plus 14 alternatives, each with few parameters: a treated-neighbour count divided by the mean degree; a two-hop walk fraction; interactions with log-degree and covariates (three variants); saturating $1-e^{-kf}$ with $k\\in\\{1,3,8\\}$; sigmoids of $f$ centred at $\\{0.25,0.5,0.75\\}$ with scale 0.1; and saturating functions of the count scaled by $\\{0.5,1,2\\}\\times$ the median degree. Exposure features are computed analytically at the observed, all-treated and all-control assignments. In the count mapping only, the all-treated feature is capped at three times the 99.5th percentile of its observed values. An alternative is kept only if its BIC is lower than the linear model's by more than 10; kept alternatives are averaged with BIC weights, and if none passes, or the average departs from the linear estimate by more than three times $\\max(|\\hat\\tau_{\\mathrm{lin}}|,\\mathrm{se}_{\\mathrm{lin}})$, the linear estimate is returned. The interval is $\\hat\\tau\\pm\\big(1.96\\,\\mathrm{se}+|\\hat\\tau-\\hat\\tau_{\\mathrm{lin}}|\\big)$, where $\\mathrm{se}$ is the BIC-weighted mean of HC1 sandwich delta-method standard errors plus the weighted spread of the kept estimates. The estimator is deterministic, reads neither the design description nor the cluster labels, and uses no resampling. It was written after inspecting model-selection results; its per-case gate decisions were not logged in verified form (the evaluation marks candidate diagnostics as unverified), so we cannot report which mapping it selected in any case. In particular, the library contains the exact sigmoid form used to generate case 6 (centre 0.5; steepness 10 corresponds to scale 0.1), so the case-6 error cannot be read as absence of the true form from the library.\n170:These contrasts are consistent with the hypothesis that the mismatch between the generating form and a fraction-linear mapping, rather than graph family, strength or assignment, is what moves the error, and they do not support a simple story in which heavier-tailed degree distributions or stronger spillovers alone degrade regression GATE estimation in this range. They do not establish it: the grid is not factorial, the three cases with higher error are also the three that depart from the linear fraction form, each pair changes more than one property at a time in at least two instances, and we did not record which mapping the estimator selected, so a mapping mismatch is not shown for any case. Case 6 is a caution: the library contains its exact generating sigmoid, yet the error is 0.198, so a missing mapping alone cannot explain it; failure of the gate to select that mapping, averaging over several mappings, or finite-sample variance at the corners are all possible and untested. The oracle's plane mean of 0.045 is similar to the per-case errors in cases 1--5, suggesting residual sampling noise at $n=3000$, though this compares a plane mean with per-case values.\n228:\\paragraph{Threats to validity.} The library includes the exact case-6 sigmoid and saturating and count-based shapes close to the grid's other forms, so any gain over linear regression partly reflects a match between the library and the grid. A practitioner whose true mechanism lies outside the library would not be protected by the gate; the fallback is then plain linear regression. Relative error divides by $|\\gate|\\ge0.5$ and is capped at 2, so it treats heavy-tailed errors differently from absolute error.\n230:\\paragraph{Limitations.} (1) Case specifications are shared between model selection and final scoring, and the library was designed after seeing model-selection per-case errors; the final result is out-of-sample over seeds but not over mechanisms. (2) There is a single graph size ($n=3000$) and a single assignment probability (0.5). (3) Contrasts are single case pairs, not a factorial design; they are not accompanied by per-case intervals, and several pairs vary more than one property. (4) Per-case values for the baselines, the bias, and the coverage are unavailable, so we make no per-case baseline comparison. (5) The cause of the larger errors in cases 6--8 (including the two-hop failure) and of the undercoverage is not established, and the mapping selected per case was not verified, so the mapping-mismatch account is a hypothesis. (6) There was no ablation of the gate margin or library components, so we cannot attribute the gain over linear regression to any particular mapping or to the gate itself; only three estimator classes were scored once on the model-selection seeds, and their per-case results are not used. (7) All mechanisms are synthetic; we ran no experiment on public graphs (e.g., Facebook100), real outcomes, learned graph representations, or other designs. (8) Qualification of the final evaluation is a statement about the measured result on this grid.\n15\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent 575027 Nov 11 09:00 paper.pdf\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent  49112 Nov 11 08:53 paper.tex\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   9051 Nov 11 08:34 references.bib","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"17d1940c-d75a-4636-9395-828d90015947","uuid":"a79aebd6-ab7a-4e3d-834e-54d7fa839555","timestamp":"2026-11-11T09:02:07.309Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Agentic evidence audit feedback\n\n- generated_at: `2026-11-11T09:01:56.230600+00:00`\n- ok: `False`\n- verdict: `fail`\n- finding_count: `2`\n- audit_json_path: `work/results/t09/claude/20261111_071431/paper/paper_evidence_audit.json`\n- audit_markdown_path: `work/results/t09/claude/20261111_071431/paper/paper_evidence_audit.md`\n- raw_output_path: `work/results/t09/claude/20261111_071431/paper/paper_evidence_audit_raw.txt`\n\n## Summary\nAll numeric results, source-code descriptions and citations match the evidence trail, but two narrative claims go beyond what the grounded evidence supports.\n\n## Findings\n- `threats-gain-attribution-1` (Discussion and Limitations (Threats to validity)): This asserts as fact a causal attribution of the 0.180-to-0.126 gain to library-grid matching. The manuscript says elsewhere that it cannot attribute the gain to any mapping or to the gate. No ablation was run, per-case baseline values are unavailable, and per-case gate decisions are unverified.\n  - claim: The library includes the exact case-6 sigmoid and saturating and count-based shapes close to the grid's other forms, so any gain over linear regression partly reflects a match between the library and the grid.\n  - evidence: paper.tex Limitation