ther than graph family, strength or assignment, is what moves the error, and they do not support a simple story in which heavier-tailed degree distributions or stronger spillovers alone degrade regression GATE estimation in this range. They do not establish it: the grid is not factorial, the three cases with higher error are also the three that depart from the linear fraction form, each pair changes more than one property at a time in at least two instances, and we did not record which mapping the estimator selected, so a mapping mismatch is not shown for any case. Case 6 is a caution: the library contains its exact generating sigmoid, yet the error is 0.198, so a missing mapping alone cannot explain it; failure of the gate to select that mapping, averaging over several mappings, or finite-sample variance at the corners are all possible and untested. The oracle's plane mean of 0.045 is similar to the per-case errors in cases 1--5, suggesting residual sampling noise at $n=3000$, though this compares a plane mean with per-case values.\n228:\\paragraph{Threats to validity.} The library includes the exact case-6 sigmoid and saturating and count-based shapes close to the grid's other forms, so any gain over linear regression partly reflects a match between the library and the grid. A practitioner whose true mechanism lies outside the library would not be protected by the gate; the fallback is then plain linear regression. Relative error divides by $|\\gate|\\ge0.5$ and is capped at 2, so it treats heavy-tailed errors differently from absolute error.\n230:\\paragraph{Limitations.} (1) Case specifications are shared between model selection and final scoring, and the library was designed after seeing model-selection per-case errors; the final result is out-of-sample over seeds but not over mechanisms. (2) There is a single graph size ($n=3000$) and a single assignment probability (0.5). (3) Contrasts are single case pairs, not a factorial design; they are not accompanied by